{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788130141,"nonce":"gYjC9NrfjhyTbAzHu29Gru4Eq5M=","message":"DELIVERED — 1.4 worker, branch `spec/1.4-rwa-cli`, three commits, no C# touched\nso no `Build:` commit is owed.\n\n    rwa/rwa           the client (single file, stdlib python3, no deps)\n    rwa/fakebench.py  a synthetic bench: Poller.cs without the game\n    rwa/selftest.sh   119 checks, the reproducible acceptance evidence\n    rwa/README.md     the drive-it manual (root, syntax, jq recipes, transcripts, watch)\n    README.md         corrected: rwa no longer said to live in rimworld-tools\n    .gitignore        transcripts/, __pycache__/\n\nLanded in THIS repo as `rwa/` per comment #4, not in rimworld-tools.\nResolutions to the spec's open questions (and five more the build forced) are\nin the preceding comment.\n\n\n`rwa/selftest.sh` — 119 passed, 0 failed, ~90s, no game involved. Re-runnable:\n\n    cd rwa \u0026\u0026 ./selftest.sh          # KEEP=1 leaves the synthetic root behind\n\nIt covers, with the real output shown inline for each: root resolution across\nboth bench layouts; all five liveness verdicts and their messages; that a\ncommand is NOT written to a dead bench's inbox; stale-on-restart over a planted\ninbox file; the ping/status/version round trip; argument syntax including\nnesting, repetition, forced types and the id rejection; the error taxonomy one\ncode at a time (unknown-op, bad-args, bad-json, no-active-game, busy,\nstale-on-restart) plus pause interrupting an advance and off-thread verbs still\nanswering during one; the client-side failures (timeout, mangled result, the\nbench dying mid-command); journal and tail; transcripts and replay; the\ndocumented jq pipelines run exactly as the README prints them; and the watch\nfps cap.\n\nAgainst the REAL bench, read-only, nothing sent (the parallel 2.6 worker owns\nthe game):\n\n- `rwa root` derives the live root with no env set.\n- `rwa status` reads the live `status.json` and grades it OK, 30.0 fps.\n- `rwa journal --list` / `--sid 20260830T214611` parse the real journal;\n  the type histogram over that session is 6 alert_on, 1 letter, 1 message,\n  3 session, 8 warning.\n- `rwa watch` (bare = report, no toggle, no write) found the live window:\n  `RimWorld by Ludeon Studios` on `special:rwagent`, hidden, cap 30.\n- The pretty renderer was run over the shipped `results/d1.json` — a real\n  2.1 digest — and rendered every field including the ones fakebench does not\n  produce (`food_needers`, `gain_w`, `kinds`, `changed.counts`). That is the\n  no-verb-knowledge claim tested against real serializer output.\n\n\nStated in the words the dispatch asked for:\n\n- **The live round-trip (ping / status / advance against a running bench) is\n  NOT demonstrated.** No command was ever written to the real bench's inbox.\n- **The `rwa watch` Hyprland + mangohud demo is NOT demonstrated.** Only the\n  read-only half was exercised live; `watch on|off` was tested against a\n  synthetic bench directory with `HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE` unset, so the\n  fps-cap file write is proven and the workspace toggle is not. Toggling\n  `special:rwagent` on Dorian's live desktop is a side effect a test may not\n  have, and with the bench window already parked there an empty reveal would\n  have covered his screen.\n\nBoth are the orchestrator's to run. Suggested sequence, ~2 minutes:\n\n    rwa/rwa status --sample 3       # ok + a real tick delta while unpaused\n    rwa/rwa ping                    # the full inbox -\u003e safe point -\u003e result loop\n    rwa/rwa verbs                   # what the running mod registers\n    rwa/rwa advance --ticks 3000    # progress on stderr, one result at the halt\n    rwa/rwa journal --since \u003cseq\u003e   # what the journal saw during it\n    rwa/rwa watch on ; rwa/rwa watch off\n    ls transcripts/\u003csid\u003e/           # the whole thing recorded\n    rwa/rwa replay \u003csid\u003e            # and re-sendable\n\nAlso not demonstrated: the Windows bench path (derived and listed, never\nexercised — there is no Windows box here), and the Xvfb fallback (`rwa watch`\ndetects a missing `RimWorldLinux` window and points at localhost:5900 per\nFINDINGS 8, but no Xvfb run was made).\n\n\nRead from the code, as instructed. None of these blocked the work; all of them\nare things a reader of the docs alone would get wrong.\n\n1. **The result envelope carries three fields DESIGN.md does not mention.**\n   DESIGN §Protocol says `{\"id\",\"op\",\"ok\":true,\"data\":{…}}`. `Poller.WriteResult`\n   also appends `\"state\":{gameLoaded,tick,paused}`, `\"sid\"` and `\"ts\"` to every\n   result, success or failure — its own comment records that as a 1.1\n   open-question resolution that never reached DESIGN. Additive and useful (the\n   client shows `sid` and uses `ts`), but the doc is behind the code.\n\n2. **`advance` has no `condition` matcher, and has a `threat` one.** DESIGN\n   §Time model advertises `until:{letter|alert|event-match|condition}`.\n   `TimeDriver` implements `letter | threat | alert | event`. `condition` does\n   not exist; `threat` (a letter with def ThreatBig/ThreatSmall) is not in\n   DESIGN's list. 4.2's \"until-guards\" and 5.1's advance specs will both hit\n   this.\n\n3. **`cannot-pause` is a shipped error code that appears in no document.**\n   `TimeDriver.Start` returns it when `CurTimeSpeed` refuses `Paused` (cutscene\n   or landing confirmation). Legal under DESIGN's \"+ per-verb codes\", but\n   nothing enumerates it, so nothing downstream will expect it.\n\n4. **`error.detail` is often JSON `null`.** `Result.Fail` defaults it, and\n   `bad-json` is raised with no detail at all. Any consumer doing\n   `.error.detail | length` needs to handle null. (`rwa` does.)\n\n5. **`status.json`'s `activeOp` can in practice only ever be `null` or\n   `advance:\u003cid\u003e`.** `GameComponentUpdate` calls `PublishSnapshot()` BEFORE\n   `DrainCommands()`, and `DrainCommands` sets and clears `activeOp` within a\n   single frame — so the snapshot published each frame always reads it as null\n   except for the advance case, which `TimeDriver.Active` supplies separately.\n   Not a defect; it does mean status.json cannot be used to watch a short\n   command in flight, and 4.2 should not try.\n\n6. **The result file is named from the envelope's `id`, not from the command\n   file's name.** `ScanInbox` uses the filename only as a fallback id, and\n   `WriteResult` names the file `Sanitize(id)` — mapping anything outside\n   `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` to `_`. A client that lets those two differ, or that uses a\n   dotted id, polls a path that never appears. `rwa` keeps them equal and\n   rejects such an id up front with that explanation.\n\n7. **The minimum round trip is ~0.25–1.0s, for every verb.** `PollMs = 500`\n   plus `MinFileAgeMs = 250` (the writer-finished heuristic, keyed on last-write\n   time, which a rename preserves). Nothing says this anywhere; 5.1's watchdogs\n   and 4.2's cadence should both be sized knowing it.\n\n8. **`ScanInbox` globs `*.json`**, so a client's temp file must not end in\n   `.json`. `rwa` writes `commands/.rwa-\u003cid\u003e.tmp` and renames.\n\n9. **JOURNAL.md nit:** the `| dev | …` table row at line 44 sits below the \"Log\n   hooks attach when…\" paragraph, outside the table it belongs to, so it renders\n   as a stray line. One-line fix, 1.2's file, not touched here.\n\n\nNothing here changes either spec's contract; both were read before deciding.\nThe surfaces they will consume are: the envelope passthrough (`data` is the\nmod's, verbatim), `--args-json` for exact command objects, the five exit codes,\nthe `rwa-` prefix on client-side error codes, and `transcripts/\u003csid\u003e/` with\n`NNN-\u003cop\u003e/cmd.json` + `result.json` + `log.ndjson`. 4.2's two existing\nassumptions — `rwa status` at session start, and `rwa watch` reporting status\nwhen watched — are both satisfied as written (bare `rwa watch` reports and\nmutates nothing).","files":null}]}